module v2.test.claim.coercion.two_algebra_fork

data live_tree_disposition: LiveTreeDisposition = SubstrateInputsOnly

// ONE QUESTION -- may this value cross from one model to another -- ANSWERED BY TWO UNRELATED
// ALGEBRAS, and until this witness nothing joined them.
//
//   (a) THE NAME-KEYED ALGEBRA. std.coercion dag_can_cast folds over two authored rosters of
//       (from_type, to_type) SPELLINGS: dag_cast_rules and grounded_primitive_coproduct_identities.
//       This side executes and is load-bearing -- v1.compiler.infer consults it at argument-versus-
//       formal admission, at literal admission, and in the type-relation fold, and
//       v1.compiler.coercion builds on it.
//   (b) THE HOMOMORPHISM ALGEBRA. v2.std.coercion coercion_fold asks v2.std.find_witness
//       find_witness for a structure-preserving witness from the source node into a candidate set,
//       and returns a CoercionResult carrying a CoercionWitness and a CoercionQuality. This is the
//       form DESIGN section 4 specifies -- coercion as a homomorphism check, one procedure asked
//       sideways. It has NO established executing consumer in the corpus today; that blank is a
//       fact this witness records rather than one it repairs.
//
// WHY THIS IS ASKABLE WITH NO NEW CAPABILITY: both are pure folds in the interpreted corpus, so one
// supplied population of source-and-target pairs reaches both. Nothing is emitted, nothing beneath
// the two folds is executed to produce the inputs (DESIGN section 3, the witness standing rule).
//
// NEITHER FOLD IS THE ORACLE FOR THE OTHER, so each row carries TWO independently specified
// expectations, and the claims below hold each algebra to its OWN contract. The agreement relation
// is read off the two expectation columns of a row rather than asserted by a further claim -- see
// the note above the claims for why such a claim would carry no information:
//
//   expected_name_keyed_admits -- read off the declared rosters in std.coercion. A crossing is
//       admitted exactly when a row spells it, in dag_cast_rules or in the grounded
//       primitive-to-coproduct identities. This is a transcription of the DECLARATION, so a roster
//       edit reds the first claim rather than silently moving the measured relation.
//   expected_homomorphism_admits -- read off the homomorphism contract as it is realized. The only
//       preservation rule coercion_fold applies is
//       preservation_rule_exact_structural_equality_zip_fold, whose predicate holds exactly when
//       the candidate is structurally equal to the source. So the homomorphism algebra admits a
//       crossing exactly when source and target are the SAME model, and admits it at quality
//       Identity. Nothing in that side spells a non-identity crossing at all.
//
// A row can therefore report BOTH AGREE AND BOTH ARE WRONG, or either algebra deviating from its
// own declared contract, rather than only "the two folds match".

type CoercionAdmission
  = CrossingAdmitted
  | CrossingRefused

// A ROW CARRIES THE PAIR TWICE, AND THAT IS THE FORK ITSELF MADE VISIBLE. The name-keyed algebra
// keys on a type-name STRING; the homomorphism algebra keys on an atom's node IDENTITY. There is no
// conversion between them in the corpus, so a fixture that wanted to supply one pair to both must
// author both spellings and assert by construction that they denote one model. That missing
// conversion is not an inconvenience of the witness -- it is the measurement: two authorities for
// one fact share no key.
type CoercionForkFixture {
  label: Symbol
  source_type: String
  target_type: String
  source_name: Symbol
  target_name: Symbol
  expected_name_keyed: CoercionAdmission
  expected_homomorphism: CoercionAdmission
}

// THE POPULATION, AND WHY IT IS THE DISCRIMINATING ONE. The two algebras partition the pair space
// into four cells, and the population inhabits all four -- a witness restricted to the happy path
// would only ever see the first.
//
//   both admit        -- int_to_int, float_to_float: a declared row whose source and target are one
//                        model, the only shape on which the two algebras can coincide.
//   both refuse       -- bool_to_float: no declared row and no structural equality.
//   name-only         -- int_to_float (a declared widening), bool_to_int (a declared crossing with
//                        no structural relation at all), int_to_nat (a GROUNDED identity that the
//                        name-keyed side carries in its own second roster). Each is a crossing the
//                        executing algebra permits and the specified algebra cannot spell.
//   homomorphism-only -- string_to_string: an IDENTITY crossing, which the name-keyed roster does
//                        not carry a row for, so the executing side refuses a value crossing to its
//                        own model while the homomorphism side admits it at quality Identity. This
//                        is the sharpest cell and it is the one an agreement-only witness would
//                        never reach.
data coercion_fork_fixtures: List<CoercionForkFixture> = [
  {
    label: ^int_to_int,
    source_type: "Int",
    target_type: "Int",
    source_name: ^Int,
    target_name: ^Int,
    expected_name_keyed: CrossingAdmitted,
    expected_homomorphism: CrossingAdmitted
  },
  {
    label: ^float_to_float,
    source_type: "Float",
    target_type: "Float",
    source_name: ^Float,
    target_name: ^Float,
    expected_name_keyed: CrossingAdmitted,
    expected_homomorphism: CrossingAdmitted
  },
  {
    label: ^int_to_float,
    source_type: "Int",
    target_type: "Float",
    source_name: ^Int,
    target_name: ^Float,
    expected_name_keyed: CrossingAdmitted,
    expected_homomorphism: CrossingRefused
  },
  {
    label: ^bool_to_int,
    source_type: "Bool",
    target_type: "Int",
    source_name: ^Bool,
    target_name: ^Int,
    expected_name_keyed: CrossingAdmitted,
    expected_homomorphism: CrossingRefused
  },
  {
    label: ^int_to_nat,
    source_type: "Int",
    target_type: "Nat",
    source_name: ^Int,
    target_name: ^Nat,
    expected_name_keyed: CrossingAdmitted,
    expected_homomorphism: CrossingRefused
  },
  {
    label: ^string_to_string,
    source_type: "String",
    target_type: "String",
    source_name: ^String,
    target_name: ^String,
    expected_name_keyed: CrossingRefused,
    expected_homomorphism: CrossingAdmitted
  },
  {
    label: ^bool_to_float,
    source_type: "Bool",
    target_type: "Float",
    source_name: ^Bool,
    target_name: ^Float,
    expected_name_keyed: CrossingRefused,
    expected_homomorphism: CrossingRefused
  }
]

fn coercion_fork_model_node(name: Symbol) -> Node {
  Node {
    kind: TypeNode { connective: Atom { identity: name } },
    children: [],
    occurrence_id: OccurrenceSynthetic
  }
}

fn coercion_fork_grounding(name: Symbol) -> CanonicalGrounding {
  CanonicalGrounding {
    node: coercion_fork_model_node(name: name),
    witness: CanonicalGroundingWitness {
      structural: StructuralPropertyWitness {
        property: ^coercion_fork_fixture_structural_property,
        evidence: coercion_fork_model_node(name: name)
      },
      closedness: StructuralPropertyWitness {
        property: ^coercion_fork_fixture_candidate_set_closedness,
        evidence: coercion_fork_model_node(name: name)
      }
    }
  }
}

fn coercion_fork_candidate_set(target_name: Symbol) -> CoercionCandidateSet {
  CoercionCandidateSet {
    set: CandidateSet {
      candidates: [coercion_fork_model_node(name: target_name)],
      closedness: StructuralPropertyWitness {
        property: ^coercion_fork_fixture_candidate_set_closedness,
        evidence: coercion_fork_model_node(name: target_name)
      }
    }
  }
}

// (a) the name-keyed algebra, asked with the pair supplied as the two spellings it keys on.
fn coercion_fork_name_keyed_admission(row: CoercionForkFixture) -> CoercionAdmission {
  if dag_can_cast(
    source_type: row.source_type,
    target_type: row.target_type
  ) {
    CrossingAdmitted
  } else {
    CrossingRefused
  }
}

// (b) the homomorphism algebra, asked with the same pair supplied as a source model and a
// one-member candidate set. The declared priority names the sole candidate, so multiplicity policy
// cannot be what decides the answer.
fn coercion_fork_homomorphism_outcome(row: CoercionForkFixture) -> Outcome<CoercionResult> {
  coercion_fold(
    source: coercion_fork_grounding(name: row.source_name),
    candidates: coercion_fork_candidate_set(target_name: row.target_name),
    policy: TargetDeclaredPriority {
      priority: coercion_fork_model_node(name: row.target_name)
    }
  )
}

fn coercion_fork_homomorphism_admission(row: CoercionForkFixture) -> CoercionAdmission {
  match coercion_fork_homomorphism_outcome(row: row) {
    Accepted { value: _, diagnostics: _ } => CrossingAdmitted
    Rejected { diagnostics: _ } => CrossingRefused
  }
}

fn coercion_admission_equal(left: CoercionAdmission, right: CoercionAdmission) -> Bool {
  match left {
    CrossingAdmitted => match right {
      CrossingAdmitted => true
      CrossingRefused => false
    }
    CrossingRefused => match right {
      CrossingAdmitted => false
      CrossingRefused => true
    }
  }
}

// The name-keyed algebra against the DECLARED ROSTERS, not against the other algebra.
test fn name_keyed_coercion_matches_the_declared_cast_rosters_holds() -> Bool {
  fold(coercion_fork_fixtures, init: true, f: fn(acc, row) {
    acc && coercion_admission_equal(
      left: coercion_fork_name_keyed_admission(row: row),
      right: row.expected_name_keyed
    )
  })
}

// The homomorphism algebra against the HOMOMORPHISM CONTRACT, not against the other algebra.
test fn homomorphism_coercion_matches_the_structural_preservation_contract_holds() -> Bool {
  fold(coercion_fork_fixtures, init: true, f: fn(acc, row) {
    acc && coercion_admission_equal(
      left: coercion_fork_homomorphism_admission(row: row),
      right: row.expected_homomorphism
    )
  })
}

// An admitted crossing on the homomorphism side is an IDENTITY and never anything else: the only
// realized preservation rule is exact structural equality, so no row may come back Exact or Widened.
// This is what makes the name-only cells a missing SPELLING rather than a stricter policy.
test fn homomorphism_coercion_admits_only_identity_quality_holds() -> Bool {
  fold(coercion_fork_fixtures, init: true, f: fn(acc, row) {
    acc && match coercion_fork_homomorphism_outcome(row: row) {
      Accepted { value: result, diagnostics: _ } => match result.quality {
        Identity => true
        Exact => false
        Widened => false
      }
      Rejected { diagnostics: _ } => true
    }
  })
}

// WHY THERE IS NO CLAIM COMPARING THE TWO ALGEBRAS TO EACH OTHER, AND NO AUTHORED `AGREE` COLUMN
// (review 70065). A claim asserting name_keyed_admission(row) == homomorphism_admission(row), read
// against an authored third column, is ENTAILED by the two claims above: each pins its side to its
// own independently specified expectation, so the comparison reduces to asking whether that third
// column contradicts the first two -- a check of the fixture against itself, which reds on no
// behaviour change the first two do not already catch. DESIGN section 4b rules the check out before
// it is written, and DESIGN section 2 names the column: a third authored fact fully determined by
// two others is re-invention, not a reduction.
//
// THE MEASUREMENT SURVIVES WITHOUT IT, and this is the point rather than a concession. The
// agreement relation is DERIVED from the two expectation columns, which sit side by side on every
// row: four of the seven rows carry expectations that differ, in both directions, and that is the
// fork stated where a reader meets the population. If either algebra's admission set moves -- in
// either direction, consolidation included -- the claim that owns that side reds and the author
// must restate the row. Nothing was lost by deleting the comparison except a second spelling of a
// fact the table already carries.
